WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.482
Back in the restaurant, a long night is over.

2
00:00:05.482 --> 00:00:08.170
A customer says the soup took forty minutes.

3
00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:10.369
The cook remembers making it in six.

4
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:14.318
And here's the awkward part: both of them are telling the truth.

5
00:00:14.318 --> 00:00:18.044
Maybe the bowl sat on the counter, waiting for the only waiter.

6
00:00:18.044 --> 00:00:20.588
Maybe the whole kitchen stopped to wash up.

7
00:00:20.588 --> 00:00:22.894
Two honest people and one bowl of soup.

8
00:00:22.894 --> 00:00:27.271
That's the hardest argument to settle, because nobody wrote anything down.

9
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.469
Our program has the same argument.

10
00:00:30.469 --> 00:00:41.081
The report service from the earlier lessons fetches each document from a document store: another program that keeps the documents and answers over the network.

11
00:00:41.081 --> 00:00:42.549
Some fetches are slow.

12
00:00:42.549 --> 00:00:44.151
So we do what we all do.

13
00:00:44.151 --> 00:00:48.756
We wrap a timer around the call, and log: fetch took 46 milliseconds.

14
00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:52.164
So here's the question that's easy to be shy about.

15
00:00:52.164 --> 00:00:54.570
The log line already has the duration.

16
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.231
What more could any tool possibly tell us?

17
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:02.633
Look at what that line can't say.

18
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:06.638
Was every thread paused right then, for a garbage collection?

19
00:01:06.638 --> 00:01:12.087
The garbage collector, or GC, is the part of the JVM that frees memory nobody uses.

20
00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:18.127
Did our thread wait for a lock, a guard that lets one thread at a time into a piece of code?

21
00:01:18.127 --> 00:01:21.279
Did something grab a pile of memory that second?

22
00:01:21.467 --> 00:01:25.428
The log line is one number, for one call, on a clock of its own.

23
00:01:25.428 --> 00:01:28.338
It's a witness who watched one table all night.

24
00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:30.937
Honest, and blind to the rest of the room.

25
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:38.505
What we'd love is one log of everything the kitchen did: every order, every wait, every wash-up, on one clock.

26
00:01:38.505 --> 00:01:43.635
And cheap enough to keep every night, because nobody knows which night will go wrong.

27
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:49.633
The JVM, the program that runs your Java and Kotlin, keeps a log like that.

28
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:55.077
It's already in your JDK, and it's called JDK Flight Recorder, or JFR.

29
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.936
Let's map the kitchen onto it.

30
00:01:57.936 --> 00:02:02.905
Each cook is a thread, a line of work that runs side by side with the others.

31
00:02:02.905 --> 00:02:13.940
Each line in the log book is an event: a small record with a start time, often a duration, the thread, and usually a stack trace, the chain of method calls that led there.

32
00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:19.024
The whole kitchen washing up is a GC pause, and it hits every thread at once.

33
00:02:19.024 --> 00:02:22.136
Waiting for the only waiter is a wait for a lock.

34
00:02:22.136 --> 00:02:28.297
And the JVM writes plenty more on its own, such as samples of what the running threads are doing.

35
00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:34.290
Our own code can add lines too, like the cook's own notes: one for every call to the store.

36
00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:37.490
And all of them sit on one timeline, on one clock.

37
00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:38.961
That's the whole point.

38
00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:45.382
Here the kitchen picture has a limit.

39
00:02:45.382 --> 00:02:47.384
The log covers our kitchen only.

40
00:02:47.384 --> 00:02:52.327
The document store is another program, like the farm that grows the vegetables.

41
00:02:52.327 --> 00:02:56.018
If the farm is slow, our log shows us waiting, but not why.

42
00:02:56.018 --> 00:02:58.396
The farm would need a book of its own.

43
00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:02.440
One session of this log is called a recording.

44
00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.578
It keeps events in memory, and moves them to disk in chunks.

45
00:03:06.578 --> 00:03:11.131
Picture a log book that tears out its oldest pages when it's full.

46
00:03:11.131 --> 00:03:13.407
maxage says how far back to keep.

47
00:03:13.407 --> 00:03:15.339
maxsize says how many bytes.

48
00:03:15.339 --> 00:03:18.443
Set neither, and the JVM keeps 250 megabytes.

49
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:25.571
You can start a recording when the JVM starts, with one option on the java command.

50
00:03:25.571 --> 00:03:32.710
Or on a JVM that's already running, with jcmd, a JDK tool that sends commands to a running JVM.

51
00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.716
Here's what the report service answered.

52
00:03:35.867 --> 00:03:41.687
And when something goes wrong, JFR.dump copies the pages written so far into a file.

53
00:03:41.687 --> 00:03:42.865
It stops nothing.

54
00:03:42.865 --> 00:03:47.023
You don't have to close the restaurant to read the log book.

55
00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.052
That's why a recording that's always on pays off.

56
00:03:50.052 --> 00:03:53.720
When the bad night comes, the last hour is already in the book.

57
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.180
But always on only works if it's cheap.

58
00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.430
JFR comes with two settings files.

59
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:05.979
default calls itself safe for continuous use in production, typically less than 1 percent overhead.

60
00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:09.155
profile says around 2 percent, and records more.

61
00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:16.122
default samples running threads every 20 milliseconds, and keeps lock waits of 20 milliseconds or more.

62
00:04:16.122 --> 00:04:19.746
profile samples every 10, and keeps lock waits from 10.

63
00:04:19.746 --> 00:04:23.305
So: default to leave on, and profile for a short hunt.

64
00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.962
Now let's ask a real recording what's in it.

65
00:04:26.962 --> 00:04:31.043
The lesson's demo runs four threads for 4 seconds, under profile.

66
00:04:31.043 --> 00:04:38.765
Each one counts words, asks a fake document store for a document, and now and then holds a shared lock for 15 milliseconds.

67
00:04:38.765 --> 00:04:41.779
Which kind of event will have the highest count?

68
00:04:41.779 --> 00:04:45.545
And how many samples of running code will 4 seconds give us?

69
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:54.170
jfr summary lists every event type, with a count.

70
00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.409
Here's part of one run.

71
00:04:56.409 --> 00:05:02.250
At the top, the garbage collector's paperwork: 1,951 events.

72
00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.073
Then ours: 1,361 store calls.

73
00:05:05.073 --> 00:05:09.551
Then 1,207 allocation samples, and 560 sleeps.

74
00:05:09.733 --> 00:05:12.840
Only the garbage collector writes more than we do.

75
00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.449
Every kitchen has one colleague like that.

76
00:05:15.449 --> 00:05:20.420
And it's good news, because its lines sit right next to ours, on the same clock.

77
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.423
Now look near the bottom.

78
00:05:22.423 --> 00:05:26.288
Execution samples, the snapshots of running code: 40.

79
00:05:26.288 --> 00:05:29.132
Just 40, in 4 seconds, on four threads.

80
00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:29.965
Why so few?

81
00:05:29.965 --> 00:05:38.286
The threads spent most of their time asleep, waiting for the store, and the sampler only counts threads that are running Java code.

82
00:05:38.286 --> 00:05:41.208
A cook waiting for the delivery isn't cooking.

83
00:05:41.208 --> 00:05:42.541
Keep that 40 in mind.

84
00:05:43.467 --> 00:05:44.545
So, a few rules.

85
00:05:44.545 --> 00:05:48.455
Set maxage or maxsize, or the book grows to 250 megabytes.

86
00:05:48.455 --> 00:05:52.837
Use default to leave it on, and profile for short investigations.

87
00:05:52.837 --> 00:05:55.938
Don't expect it to see inside another program.

88
00:05:55.938 --> 00:06:03.893
And 40 samples are enough to spot a method that takes most of the time, but far too few to see what happens inside it.

89
00:06:03.893 --> 00:06:08.275
That takes a profiler with thousands, and that's the next lesson.

90
00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.976
So, what can a recording tell you that the log line can't?

91
00:06:12.976 --> 00:06:19.746
What else happened at the same moment: a GC pause, a lock wait, a burst of allocation, all on one clock.

92
00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:23.913
A cheap recording that's always on is evidence you already have.

93
00:06:23.913 --> 00:06:25.931
Dump it, instead of restarting.

94
00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:26.987
Quick check.

95
00:06:26.987 --> 00:06:29.900
A request was slow at 9:14 last night.

96
00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:35.266
The program has run a recording all week, with maxage set to one hour.

97
00:06:35.266 --> 00:06:36.416
It's now 10:30.

98
00:06:36.416 --> 00:06:38.409
Is 9:14 still in the book?

99
00:06:41.667 --> 00:06:41.876
No.

100
00:06:41.876 --> 00:06:46.422
The book only keeps the last hour, so 9:14 was torn out at 10:14.

101
00:06:46.422 --> 00:06:50.967
Keep enough of the book to cover the time it takes you to notice.
